**New Evidence**
- Ethiopia’s anti-corruption committee was formally launched by Abiy in November 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gov’t Establishes National Committee To Coordinate Anti-Corruption Campaign","publisher":"Ethiopian News Agency","publication_date":"2022-11-17","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_40240","claim":"Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed announced a national anti-corruption committee, said corruption had become a security threat, and said the committee would bring culprits before court.","locator":"lines 55-63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Official government reporting with direct attribution to Abiy; strong for the fact of the announcement, weaker for effectiveness or neutrality.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in the 2022 target period","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Abiy as PM; shows his stated anti-corruption posture and an executive action he ordered.","contrary_evidence":["Self-report does not establish unbiased enforcement or real effectiveness","Other 2022 reporting still described widespread corruption and weak accountability"],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- Abiy publicly framed the judiciary, prosecutors, police, and auditors as corrupt and called for reform.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM’s Comment On Judges Offends Association","publisher":"The Reporter Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-06-18","url":"https://www.thereporterethiopia.com/24384/","claim":"Abiy told parliament judges were the number one thieves, described the justice system as a sanctuary of thieves, and said prosecutors, police, and auditors were also corrupt while promising serious reform.","locator":"lines 158-183","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local reporting with direct quotations from the parliamentary address, but not an independent transcript or audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct public statement by Abiy; it supports candor about systemic corruption, though it could also be read as political scapegoating.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is denunciatory and not itself proof of truthful disclosure","No independent finding here proves the alleged corruption claims were accurate in the same form"],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- Abiy filled the federal auditor-general post in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Executive Is Happy When Taking Budget, But Unhappy When They See Our Auditors,","publisher":"The Reporter Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-12-24","url":"https://www.thereporterethiopia.com/28896/","claim":"The article says Meseret Damtie was chosen by Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed in June 2022 to head the Office of the Federal Auditor General, a post that had been vacant for one year, and that she brought long public-finance experience to the role.","locator":"lines 158-160","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Local interview/profile with specific appointment details, but still a secondary source and not a nomination record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"The appointment is attributed to Abiy as PM; it is relevant to whether he empowered a professional oversight office rather than leaving it vacant.","contrary_evidence":["Appointment quality does not prove independence in practice","The same interview says executive institutions often do not provide documents promptly to auditors"],"lenses":["7B.5","7B.6","7B.7"]}

- The auditor-general described delayed access to the PM’s own project as an oversight gap.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Executive Is Happy When Taking Budget, But Unhappy When They See Our Auditors,","publisher":"The Reporter Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-12-24","url":"https://www.thereporterethiopia.com/28896/","claim":"The auditor-general said she learned about the PM’s Chaka Project only after Abiy informed parliament and said the project would likely be audited when the PMO is audited.","locator":"lines 302-305","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_low","source_confidence_reason":"Same interview is detailed and specific, but the claim is indirect and does not itself prove personal enrichment or concealment.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"The project is PM-led, so the oversight gap is attributable to the prime minister’s office, but it does not by itself show self-dealing.","contrary_evidence":["The project was described as public rather than personal","No audit finding here proves improper funding or hidden benefit"],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- Ethiopian authorities detained journalists without formal charges, targeting critical outlets.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Journalists Yayesew Shimelis and Temesgen Desalegn detained in Ethiopia","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-05-27","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/05/journalists-yayesew-shimelis-and-temesgen-desalegn-detained-in-ethiopia/","claim":"CPJ said Ethiopian authorities detained journalists from critical outlets and did not file formal charges or allege specific laws, while the arrests were part of a broader crackdown.","locator":"lines 523-525","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous reporting from a leading press-freedom watchdog with specific cases and named detainees.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"The detentions were by Ethiopian authorities under Abiy’s government; attribution is strongest for tolerance/permission of the crackdown rather than a proven personal order.","contrary_evidence":["Government spokespeople denied the detentions were work-related","Some detainees were eventually released or later moved through court"],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- Reuters reported the rights body wanted 16 journalists released and said the government was intimidating the media.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopian rights body seeks release of 16 detained journalists","publisher":"Al Jazeera / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-05-27","url":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/5/27/ethiopian-rights-body-seeks-release-of-16-detained-journalists","claim":"Reuters reported that Ethiopia’s state-appointed rights body called for the release of 16 journalists, said the government was increasingly intimidating the media and harassing opponents, and noted that authorities justified arrests by accusing journalists of siding with rebels.","locator":"lines 78-84 and 92","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting carried by Al Jazeera with named official body and specific arrest context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"This is attributable to Abiy’s government as the governing authority behind the arrests and media warning, though the rights body itself is independent evidence.","contrary_evidence":["The government said it would act against illegal activity and denied media-based arrests","The statement does not prove personal bad faith beyond the administration’s conduct"],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- CPJ said the government’s war narrative and detentions were suppressing independent reporting.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Journalists face growing hostility as Ethiopia’s civil war persists","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-08-01","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/08/journalists-face-growing-hostility-as-ethiopias-civil-war-persists/","claim":"CPJ said the government insisted on calling the conflict a 'law enforcement operation' rather than a war, documented at least 63 arrests of journalists and media workers since November 2020, and said the climate had become increasingly hostile to independent reporting.","locator":"lines 522-527","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed watchdog analysis with a documented arrest count and a direct description of the state narrative.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers 2022 and the ongoing 2022 crackdown","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is to Abiy’s administration rather than a proven personal directive; it matters because he was the chief executive during the narrative and arrest campaign.","contrary_evidence":["The government framed the conflict as law enforcement and said arrests were for crimes","Openly contested narratives can exist in wartime, so the statement alone does not prove deliberate lying"],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- Freedom House described pressure, license revocations, and imprisonment without charges.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia: Freedom in the World 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/ethiopia/freedom-world/2022","claim":"Freedom House said Ethiopian journalists and media networks came under government pressure over conflict coverage, some were expelled and had licenses revoked, several journalists were imprisoned without charges, and the Media Authority banned local broadcasters from retransmitting foreign media programs.","locator":"lines 175-176","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Established country report from a major democracy watchdog, summarizing the target environment with specific examples.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"published in 2022 and describing the 2021-22 political environment","ruler_attribution":"The actions are attributed to Abiy’s government as the national executive in control of media licensing and security policy.","contrary_evidence":["The same report says Abiy had initially opened media space after 2018","Not every media restriction proves personal dishonesty rather than authoritarian governance"],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- HRW said accountability efforts lacked transparency and independent oversight.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Ethiopia","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/ethiopia","claim":"HRW said government efforts toward accountability for abuses were inadequate and lacked transparency and independent oversight, while journalists, civil society organizations, and outspoken public figures faced an increasingly hostile and restrictive environment.","locator":"lines 71-75","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights organization’s annual country chapter with explicit language about oversight and reporting conditions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers events of 2022 and the November truce period","ruler_attribution":"The accountability failures are attributed to the federal government led by Abiy; this is strongest for obstruction/tolerance, not a proven personal profit motive.","contrary_evidence":["HRW also notes the November ceasefire and later accountability mechanisms under discussion","Some abuses were committed by non-state or regional actors, so not all wrongdoing is personally attributable"],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- Abiy ended the emergency powers earlier than scheduled.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia's parliament lifts state of emergency early","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-02-16","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/02/16/us-ethiopia-conflict","claim":"Reuters reported that parliament ended a six-month state of emergency early; the emergency had allowed detention without charge and warrantless home searches, and lifting it curtailed those extraordinary powers.","locator":"lines 603-612","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting gives a precise procedural account of the emergency rollback and its legal effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"The cabinet had approved the early end and Abiy’s government was the author of the emergency regime, so the restraint is attributable to the administration.","contrary_evidence":["The emergency had already enabled mass detention and search powers","Ending the emergency does not erase prior abuse"],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.8"]}

- Abiy publicly committed to implementing the Tigray peace deal.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia's Abiy Says He's Committed to Implementing Tigray Peace Deal","publisher":"Voice of America","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://www.voanews.com/a/ethiopia-s-abiy-says-committed-to-implementing-tigray-peace-deal-/6835201.html","claim":"Abiy told lawmakers that the parties had discussed, agreed, and signed the peace deal and that the next step was to implement it, adding that the cease-fire required unhindered aid access to Tigray.","locator":"lines 94-108","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear direct quotation in contemporaneous reporting, though still a secondary source rather than a transcript.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Abiy’s public commitment before parliament; relevant as a promise/commitment record rather than proof of truthfulness.","contrary_evidence":["Subsequent reporting still documented implementation gaps and continuing conflict pressures","A promise of implementation is not evidence that the promise was kept"],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.8","7B.10"]}

- A New Yorker profile reported personal fundraising and donation behavior.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Did a Nobel Peace Laureate Stoke a Civil War?","publisher":"The New Yorker","publication_date":"2022-09-26","url":"https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2022/10/03/did-a-nobel-peace-laureate-stoke-a-civil-war","claim":"The profile says Abiy held fund-raisers for civic projects and that, in 2020, his wife’s office solicited donations for schools while Abiy gave about two million dollars in book profits to build more.","locator":"lines 139-140","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_low","source_confidence_reason":"A well-edited magazine profile, but still narrative reporting rather than a financial audit or disclosure filing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"covers the target-period reputation and ongoing civic-project behavior in 2022","ruler_attribution":"The donation and fundraising behavior are directly attributed to Abiy and his office; it is a positive ethical example, but one piece of narrative reporting is not enough to establish a broad integrity record.","contrary_evidence":["The same profile is critical of Abiy’s broader rule and war record","Personal giving does not negate possible hidden conflicts elsewhere"],"lenses":["7B.10","7B.3"]}

- The government itself announced arrests in the anti-corruption drive.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nat'l Anti-Corruption Committee Detaining Suspects for Corrupt Practices","publisher":"Ethiopian News Agency","publication_date":"2022-12-02","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_40822","claim":"The National Anti-Corruption Committee said it had started arresting individuals for alleged corrupt practices, including officials accused of financial plundering and land grabbing.","locator":"lines 53-55","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Official government news source reporting enforcement actions, but the underlying cases are not independently adjudicated in the article.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"This reflects an anti-corruption campaign authorized by Abiy’s government; it helps show action, but not necessarily clean or even-handed enforcement.","contrary_evidence":["The article reports allegations and arrests, not convictions or due-process review","Selective anti-corruption sweeps can be politicized"],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I screened roughly 30 distinct documents across Reuters/Euronews, CPJ, Freedom House, HRW, VOA, ENA, The Reporter, the New Yorker, Al Jazeera/Reuters, GOV.UK, Africa Intelligence, and a few broader context items. I kept only items with a direct ruler-nexus or a clear, attributable institutional action.

Rejected or not-accepted items included:
- Generic corruption indicators and governance priors such as WGI, V-Dem, CPI, and other country-level measures, because they are context only and not personal-conduct evidence.
- Purely economic or humanitarian materials from IMF, World Bank, UNICEF, WFP, and conflict reports unless they directly tied to Abiy’s own statements, appointments, concealment, or oversight choices.
- Several conflict-and-security pieces that were strong for 3B/4B but too indirect for 7B unless they also showed media suppression, obstruction of scrutiny, or a ruler-level accountability choice.
- 2026 retrospective profile fragments and background election/video materials that were either out of period or too inferential for 2022 integrity.
- Asset-declaration and legal-framework pages that were useful context but did not show Abiy’s own filing, concealment, or personal benefit.
- Opinion pieces and commentary that alleged bad faith without a traceable factual core or independent corroboration.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct, audited evidence on Abiy’s personal assets, family wealth, gifts, campaign finance, or hidden business interests is still thin.
- I did not find a strong 2022 source proving direct self-enrichment, kickbacks, or a family conflict of interest.
- Evidence is strongest on media suppression, punishment of journalists, and mixed anti-corruption rhetoric, weaker on personal financial honesty.
- More search would help if you want a tighter 7B judgment on personal enrichment or a stronger read on whether the anti-corruption campaign was genuinely even-handed versus selectively enforced.
